  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "An owner positioning a hotel around **differentiated guest experience** and **local character** in the Caribbean and Latin America should look first at **lifestyle, boutique, and soft-brand platforms** that explicitly emphasize place-based design and curated experiences.[2][4][6][7][9]\n\nThe strongest brand options in your search results are:\n\n- **Canopy by Hilton** — Hilton describes it as an upscale lifestyle brand with a “local experience” and “boutique hotel feel,” designed to connect guests to the locale through layered design, food and beverage, and crafted touchpoints.[2]\n- **Kimpton Hotels & Restaurants** — IHG positions Kimpton as a **design-forward** brand known for “unique experiences,” and it is already expanding in MLAC.[4]\n- **Hotel Indigo** — IHG describes Hotel Indigo as inspired by each hotel’s surrounding **neighborhood and culture**, making it a strong fit for local-character positioning.[4]\n- **Vignette Collection** — IHG calls it a curation of distinct, hand-picked hotels offering **one-of-a-kind stays**, which suits independent-feeling assets with strong identity.[4]\n- **Tapestry Collection by Hilton** — Hilton says these hotels are celebrated for their **unique, vibrant personality** and incorporation of **local elements and experiences**.[6]\n- **Curio Collection by Hilton** — Hilton’s region activity includes Curio lifestyle hotels such as York Medellín and Zemi Miches, reflecting demand for **curated, experiential stays**.[5]\n- **The Luxury Collection** — Marriott’s positioning in the region emphasizes hotels that reflect the “**soul of their destinations**” through immersive cultural experiences, refined design, and local heritage.[7]\n- **W Hotels** — Marriott includes W among its luxury portfolio in the region, and it is commonly used for lifestyle-driven, design-led urban and resort assets.[8]\n- **Edition** — Marriott has additional Edition properties planned in the region, and the brand is generally used for highly design-focused, lifestyle-oriented positioning.[8]\n- **SLS** — Accor has used SLS in the Bahamas, and the brand is associated with upscale, contemporary lifestyle resort positioning.[9]\n- **MGallery** — Accor has signed an MGallery in Cancun; this brand is typically used for distinctive, locally rooted hotels with personality.[9]\n- **Fairmont / Sofitel** — Accor notes these higher-end brands are used for luxury and all-inclusive expansion in the region, though they are usually less “boutique-lifestyle” than the brands above.[9]\n- **Six Senses** — IHG highlights Six Senses in the region as focused on **wellness and sustainability experiences**, which can work well where destination identity and wellbeing are central to the concept.[3][4]\n\nIf you want the best-fit shortlist for an owner specifically seeking **local character first**, the clearest matches from these results are **Hotel Indigo, Canopy by Hilton, Tapestry Collection, Vignette Collection, Curio Collection, and The Luxury Collection**.[2][4][6][7] If the asset is more design-led and experiential at the upper end, **Kimpton, W, Edition, SLS, and Six Senses** are also strong candidates.[3][4][8][9]\n\nIf helpful, I can also turn this into a **brand-by-brand owner fit matrix** for resort, urban, all-inclusive, and conversion opportunities in the Caribbean and Latin America.",
